{
 "indicator": {
  "definition": {
   "definition": "Forward premia measure the annualised percentage by which the forward USD/INR rate exceeds the spot rate for a given tenor — the cost of buying dollars forward rather than spot. A positive premium means the rupee is at a forward discount to the dollar. They are computed from matched inter-bank USD/INR swap pairs (near leg = spot, far leg = forward) reported on the CCIL platform: the premium is the difference between the far-leg and near-leg rate as a percentage of the near-leg rate, annualised over the actual number of days between the two settlement dates. FBIL, the RBI-recognised benchmark administrator, publishes the premia curve, and RBI reproduces the 1-, 3- and 6-month rates in the Weekly Statistical Supplement ('Ratios and Rates' table, 'Inter-Bank Forward Premia of US Dollar'). A rise means dollars have become more expensive forward — typically a wider India-US interest-rate differential (covered interest parity), firmer expectations of rupee depreciation and/or stronger forward dollar demand from importers and hedgers.",
   "source_name": "FBIL — Methodology Document for the FBIL Forward Premia Curve and MIFOR Curve; reproduced in RBI's Weekly Statistical Supplement",
   "source_url": "https://www.fbil.org.in/uploads/REVISED_ETHODOLOGY_FOR_FORWARD_PREMIA_AND_MIFOR_CURVE_4b25a32468.pdf",
   "term": "US dollar forward premia (1, 3 and 6 months), per cent per annum"
  },
